 graphs are proposed.\n\n## Section snippets\n\n## Introduction and preliminaries\n\nRegulations for channel assignments of frequency modulation transmitters have motivated the introduction of several graph theory models. The goal of these models is to fulfill the condition that two radio stations that are located sufficiently close to each other broadcast on channels that are sufficiently far apart since otherwise interference of their signals may occur. In general, a graph-theoretical approach that assigns radio frequencies to radio stations is modeled by labelings of\n\n## Theoretical bounds\n\nWe first present the result that improve the existing upper bounds on the k$k$-labeling number of D(1,2,\\dots ,t)$D(1,2,\\dots ,t)$.\n\nWe will need the following two results.\n\nTheorem 1\n\n## [14, Theorem 2]\n\nIf t\\ge 2$t\\ge 2$ is an integer and k$k$ an even integer, then\n\nr{l}_{k}\\left(D(1,2,\\dots ,t)\\right)\\le t\\frac{{k}^{2}}{2}+k$r{l}_{k}\\left(D(1,2,\\dots ,t)\\right)\\le t\\frac{{k}^{2}}{2}+k$.\n\nTheorem 2\n\n## [14, Theorem 3]\n\nIf t\\ge 2$t\\ge 2$ is an integer and k$k$ an odd integer, then\n\nr{l}_{k}\\left(D(1,2,\\dots ,t)\\right)\\le t\\frac{{k}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{t}{2}k$r{l}_{k}\\left(D(1,2,\\dots ,t)\\right)\\le t\\frac{{k}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{t}{2}k$.\n\nIf k$k$ is odd, then the upper bound on the k$k$-labeling number of D(1,2,\\dots ,t)$D(1,2,\\dots ,t)$ can be improved as follows.\n\nTheorem 3\n\nIf t\\ge 2$t\\ge 2$ is an integer and k$k$ an odd integer, then r{l}_{k}\\left(D(1,2,\\dots ,t)\\right)\\le t\\frac{{k}^{2}+1}{2}+k-1$r{l}_{k}\\left(D(1,2,\\dots ,t)\\right)\\le t\\frac{{k}^{2}+1}{2}+k-1$.\n\nProof\n\nIt is well known, e.q. [14], that\n\n## Mathematical optimization methods\n\nWe applied two well-known techniques for computing the radio k$k$-labeling of distance graphs: Integer linear programing (ILP) and a reduction to SAT. Both methods have been successfully applied for distance constrained coloring problems for various finite and infinite graphs. See for example [13] and [12], where these concepts were used for the packing coloring problem and L(3,2,1)$L(3,2,1)$-labeling problem, respectively.\n\nLet G=(V,E)$G=(V,E)$ be a graph, while k$k$ and s$s$ are positive integers. For a vertex v\\in V\\left(G\\right)$v\\in V\\left(G\\right)$ and\n\n## Results and discussion\n\nThis section summarizes all known values and bounds on the radio k$k$-labeling problem for distance graphs D(1,2,\\dots ,t)$D(1,2,\\dots ,t)$, D(1,t)$D(1,t)$ and D(t-1,t)$D(t-1,t)$, where t\\in \\{2,\\dots ,15\\}$t\\in \\{2,\\dots ,15\\}$ and k\\in \\{2,\\dots ,9\\}$k\\in \\{2,\\dots ,9\\}$. More comprehensive presentation can be found on the web page https://omr.fnm.um.si/wp-content/uploads/2017/06/web.pdf, where results for t,k\\in \\{2,\\dots ,15\\}$t,k\\in \\{2,\\dots ,15\\}$ are shown. We applied Gurobi Optimization [6] for solving ILP models and Cryptominisat5 [11] to find the solutions of the derived propositional formulas.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) L(3, 2, 1)-labeling of graphs (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/279653460_L3_2_1-labeling_of_graphs)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Given a graph G, an L(3, 2, 1)-labeling of G is a function f from the vertex set V (G) to the set of all nonnegative integers such that |f(u) - f(v)| ≥ 1 if d(u, v) = 3, |f(u) - f(v)| ≥ 2 if d(u, v) = 2 and |f(u)-f(v)| ≥ 3 if d(u, v) = 1. ... We study the L(3, 2, 1)-labelings of graphs in this paper. ... Ma-Lian Chia 1, David Kuo 2, Hong-ya Liao 3,\nArticle PDF Available\n\n# L(3, 2, 1)-labeling of graphs\n\n  * December 2011\n  * Taiwanese Journal of Mathematics 15(6):2439-2457\n\nAuthors:\n\nMa-Lian Chia\n\n  * Aletheia University\n\nDavid Kuo\n\n  * National Dong Hwa University\n\nHong-Ya Liao\n\nHong-Ya Liao\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nC.-H. Yang\n\nC.-H. Yang\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nShow all 5 authors Hide\n\nImage\n\nDownload full-text PDFRead full-text\n\n## Abstract\n\nGiven a graph G, an L(3, 2, 1)-labeling of G is a function f from the vertex set V (G) to the set of all nonnegative integers such that |f(u) - f(v)| ≥ 1 if d(u, v) = 3, |f(u) - f(v)| ≥ 2 if d(u, v) = 2 and |f(u)-f(v)| ≥ 3 if d(u, v) = 1. For a nonnegative integer k, a k-L(3, 2, 1)-labeling is an L(3, 2, 1)-labeling such that no label is greater than k. The L(3, 2, 1)-labeling number of G, denoted by λ3,2,1(G), is the smallest number k such that G has a k-L(3, 2, 1)-labeling. We study the L(3, 2, 1)-labelings of graphs in this paper. We give upper bounds for the L(3, 2, 1)-labeling numbers of general graphs and trees, and consider the L(3, 2, 1)-labeling numbers of several classes of graphs, such as the Cartesian product of paths and cycles, and the powers of paths.\n\nImage: ResearchGate Logo\n\nDiscover the world's research\n\n  * 25+ million members\n  * 160+ million publication pages\n  * 2.3+ billion citations\nJoin for free\n\nPublic Full-text 1\n\nContent uploaded by David Kuo\n\nAuthor content\n\nAll content in this area was uploaded by David Kuo on Nov 08, 2015\n\nContent may be subject to copyright.\n\nTAIWANESE JOURNAL OF MATHEMATICS\n\nVol. 15, No. 6, pp. 2439-2457, December 2011\n\nThis paper is available online at http://tjm.math.ntu.edu.tw\n\nL(3,2,1)-LABELING OF GRAPHS\n\nMa-Lian Chia 1, David Kuo 2, Hong-ya Liao 3,\n\nCian-Hui Yang 3 and Roger K. Yeh 3\n\nAbstract. Given a graph G, an L(3,2,1)-labeling of G is a function f\n\nfrom the vertex set V(G)to the set of all nonnegative integers such that\n\n|f(u)−f(v)|1 if d(u, v)=3,|f(u)−f(v)|2 if d(u, v)=2 and\n\n|f(u)−f(v)|3 if d(u, v)=1. For a nonnegative integer k,a k-L(3,2,1)-\n\nlabeling is an L(3,2,1)-labeling such that no label is greater than k. The\n\nL(3,2,1)-labeling number of G, denoted by λ 3,2,1(G),is the smallest number\n\nk such that G has a k-L(3,2,1)-labeling. We study the L(3,2,1)-labelings of\n\ngraphs in this paper. We give upper bounds for the L(3,2,1)-labeling numbers\n\nof general graphs and trees, and consider the L(3,2,1)-labeling numbers of\n\nseveral classes of graphs, such as the Cartesian product of paths and cycles,\n\nand the powers of paths.\n\n1. I NTRODUCTION\n\nThe L(2,1)-labeling problem proposed by Griggs and Roberts [9] is a variation\n\nof the frequency assignment problem introduced by Hale [5]. Suppose we are given\n\na number of transmitters or stations. The L(2,1)-labeling problem is to assign fre-\n\nquencies (nonnegative integers) to the transmitters so that “close” transmitters must\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nL(3, 2, 1)-Labeling of the square of cycles (https://iris.uniroma1.it/handle/11573/1763025)\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; Crawled: last month; L(3, 2, 1)-Labeling of the square of cycles / Bianco, V., Calamoneri, T.. - In: THEORETICAL COMPUTER SCIENCE. - ISSN 0304-3975. - 1060:(2026).\nTo prove our bounds, we introduce the new notions of labeling schemes associated with sets of consecutive labels and of forbidden labeling schemes.\n\nL(3, 2, 1)-Labeling of the square of cycles / Bianco, V., Calamoneri, T.. - In: THEORETICAL COMPUTER SCIENCE. - ISSN 0304-3975. - 1060:(2026). [10.1016/j.tcs.2025.115627]\n\n### L(3, 2, 1)-Labeling of the square of cycles\n\n##### Bianco, Valerio;Calamoneri, Tiziana^{\n\n##### 2026\n\n#### Abstract\n\nGiven a graph G=(V,E), an L(3, 2, 1)-labeling of G consists of assigning an integer label from {0,…,σ(G)} to each node v such that it is at least 3 apart from the labels of the nodes adjacent to v, at least 2 apart from the labels of the nodes at distance 2 from v, and different from the labels of the nodes at a distance 3 from v. The L(3, 2, 1)-labeling problem consists of determining the minimum value of σ(G) for which such labeling exists; this number is denoted by λ(G) and is called the L(3, 2, 1)-labeling number of G. The problem has many possible applications in coding theory, signal processing, radar, data base management, communication network addressing, etc. and is NP-hard in general, while, it has been proved to be polynomially solvable for some classes of graphs. In this paper, we continue the study of the L(3, 2, 1)-number of the square of cycles Cn2, either closing or reducing the gap between the upper and lower bounds on the values of λ(Cn2) when n assumes several values. To prove our bounds, we introduce the new notions of labeling schemes associated with sets of consecutive labels and of forbidden labeling schemes.\n\nAnno di pubblicazione\n\n2026\n\nParole chiave\n\nConstrained node labeling; Frequency assignment problems; L(h, k)-Labeling; Square of cycles\n\nTipologia\n\n01 Pubblicazione su rivista::01a Articolo in rivista\n\nCitazione\n\nL(3, 2, 1)-Labeling of the square of cycles / Bianco, V., Calamoneri, T.. - In: THEORETICAL COMPUTER SCIENCE. - ISSN 0304-3975. - 1060:(2026). [10.1016/j.tcs.2025.115627]\n\nFile allegati a questo prodotto\n\nNon ci sono file associati a questo prodotto.\n\n#####  Informazioni\n\nUtilizza questo identificativo per citare o creare un link a questo documento: `https://hdl.handle.net/11573/1763025`--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBordeaux Graph Workshop 2012 (https://bgw2012.labri.fr/booklet.pdf)\nciteturn1search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.1 years ago; Radio Labelings of Distance Graphs\nRoman Čada 1, Jan Ekstein 1, Přemysl Holub 1, and Olivier Togni 2 ... A radio k-labeling of a connected graph G is an assignment f of non negative integers to the\nRadio Labelings of Distance Graphs\nRoman Čada 1, Jan Ekstein 1, Přemysl Holub 1, and Olivier Togni 2\n1 University of West Bohemia, Pilsen, Czech Republic\n2 LE2I, University of Bourgogne, France\nExtended Abstract\nA radio k-labeling of a connected graph G is an assignment f o